probably there's been no greater  intervention in the history of human  health than the advent and utilization  of antibiotics for cure of infectious  disease so it's had a tremendous impact  and really sparked the development of  antimicrobial agents not only against  bacteria which penicillin was the  prototype for but also now against fungi  and viruses which plague humans  throughout the world as well in 1942 and  Miller who was the wife of the athletic  director at Yale at the time was ill in  New Haven Hospital with streptococcus  infection in her bloodstream she at the  time there was no antibiotics available  and she had had fevers for several weeks  and then in an effort to try to help her  the chair of physiology John Fulton at  the time who had a relationship with  Howard Florey in the UK who was  developing penicillin for mass  distribution during World War two was  able to actually procure some penicillin  for Miss Miller through doctor floor in  the UK it was shipped to the United  States through Merck and then brought by  a special messenger to New Haven for use  in and Miller to actually arrived on a  Saturday morning she was administered  the drug for the first time and was the  first patient whose life was saved with  penicillin in the United States the next  day she had a complete reduction in her  fever and she survived to the age of 90  having passed away actually in the year  2000 after that Francis Gilman Blake who  was the Dean of the Medical School  actually initiated clinical trials on  several hundred patients with penicillin  after that and it became of course then  much more widespread use today at the  United States and since then well I  think most of the most productive  efforts in trying to reduce the global  burden and infectious disease is by  improving the diagnosis having more  rapid diagnosis  having more widespread access to  antimicrobial agents and more widespread  access to vaccines so the latter two are  certainly highlighted in the HIV  epidemic and in the epidemic of  drug-resistant tuberculosis which is  continuing to spread worldwide so our  efforts on the basis of on on behalf of  our nation and other nations in gaining  access to antiretroviral therapies  globally particularly the sub-saharan  Africa has made a huge impact and more  rapidly diagnosing HIV and diseases like  tuberculosis have made a huge impact in  reducing the total global burden of  those diseases
 

